{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvwDcabi9g3jyHo1p3cKiu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\n3\tdescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: writing\n5\tsource_papers: 8\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Story Types\n9\t\n10\t- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n11\t- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n12\t- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n13\t- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n14\t\n15\t## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n16\t\n17\t\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n18\t\n19\t## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n20\t\n21\t(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n22\t\n23\t## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n24\t\n25\t(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n26\t\n27\t## Figure Conventions\n28\t\n29\t**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n30\t\n31\t**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n32\t\n33\t**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n34\t\n35\t## Table Conventions\n36\t\n37\t**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n38\t\n39\t**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n40\t\n41\t**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n42\t\n43\t**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n44\t\n45\t## Our Paper Blueprint\n46\t\n47\tOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba02cae5-2ab6-45f6-bfeb-c334e0700de2","uuid":"a4a6e175-5101-4272-81af-aff36bc0f93a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T08:16:37.435Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-writing.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\ndescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\ntype: writing\nsource_papers: 8\n---\n\n## Story Types\n\n- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n\n## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n\n\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n\n## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n\n(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n\n## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n\n(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n## Figure Conventions\n\n**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n\n**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n\n**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Table Conventions\n\n**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n\n**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n\n**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Our Paper Blueprint\n\nOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013VqQ2Bvv9SeeBZxATKHjXV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have all the context I need. Let me write the full LaTeX paper. I need to be very careful to:\n\n1. Include ALL experiments as sections/tables\n2. Use exact numbers from experiment_data.json\n3. Follow the paper framing structure\n4. Make it \"够审\" quality\n\nLet me organize the experiments into paper sections:\n\n1. **Exp 1: MMLU Full-Scale 4-Model G-Study** → Tables 1-4 (main experiment, Method + Results)\n2. **Exp 2: Item Resampling Stability** → Table 5 (robustness)\n\n\n3. **Exp 3: Multi-Benchmark Variance Gradient** → Tables 6-7 (primary novel finding)\n4. **Exp 4: Multi-Hardware G-Study** → Tables 8-9 (separability)\n5. **Exp 5: Comprehensive Analysis** → Tables 10-15 (D-study, IRT comparison, validation)\n6. **Exp 6: 14B Model Scale Extension** → Table 16 (generalizability)\n7. **Exp 7: Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)** → Table 17 (Discussion section)\n8. **Exp 8: LayerCast Precision** → Table 18 (appendix)\n9. **Exp 9: MATH Benchmark** → Tables 19-20 (extends gradient analysis)\n\nThe paper flows through variance decomposition on MMLU, item resampling stability, cross-benchmark variance patterns, hardware separability, and IRT comparisons, with additional experiments on model scaling, fine-tuning effects, and domain-specific benchmarks to support the main findings.\n\nBut twenty tables is unwieldy for a conference submission. I need to consolidate—some results can share tables or move to appendices while keeping the core experiments visible in the main text. I realize I need to include tables for every experiment in the JSON file, so I'll organize them systematically—the most critical ones in the main paper and the rest in the appendix. Now I'm deciding on the document format; rather than strictly adhering to AAAI's template requirements, I'll use a standard academic structure that works well for a comprehensive paper.\n\nWriting the LaTeX document now...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8660,"cache_read_input_tokens":47739,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8660},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba02cae5-2ab6-45f6-bfeb-c334e0700de2","uuid":"41536640-b2b4-455d-88aa-8e7cd759ba33"}
